old_sentence:
Writing the numerator as $k^*+(k+k^*\tau_v)s+k\tau_Ts^2$, $G$ depends only on the four combinations
\begin{equation}
k^*,\qquad k+k^*\tau_v,\qquad k\,\tau_T,\qquad \tau_q ,
\end{equation}
so the parameters can move along $(\delta k,\delta\tau_T,\delta\tau_v)=(\delta,\,-\tau_T\delta/k,\,-\delta/k^*)$ without changing $G$ and hence without changing any wave property of the one-dimensional problem, which is consistent with the combination $\tau_v^{*}=k+k^*\tau_v$ of Roy Choudhuri \citep{RoyChoudhuri2007}.

